  as a constraint so a later feature can't quietly start trusting it.

  [1mThe next fork: how long each id lives.[0m The correlation id is easy and I'll propose it rather than ask — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m,
  survives restarts, survives logout, because correlating one person across sessions is the entire point of it. Say the
  word if you disagree.

  The auth identity is the real decision, and it's the one that's awkward to change later because every reader has to
  cope with the id disappearing at a different moment:

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — survives navigation between pages in the tab, dies when the tab closes. Given "other forms will
    need it later" and that this is a multi-page static site today, this is the only option where a second form can
    actually read it without re-login. Moderate exposure: it's on disk-ish for the tab's life.
  - [1mIn-memory only[0m — safest by a wide margin, nothing persisted anywhere. But it dies on every page navigation, so with
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m plus future form pages the user re-authenticates constantly. Only workable if the app becomes a single
    page.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m with explicit expiry[0m — survives browser restart, so returning users stay logged in. Largest exposure
    window, and it only works if we also build expiry checking and a logout that reliably clears it. Undoing this later
    means dealing with ids already sitting in users' browsers.

  I recommend [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, paired with an explicit logout that clears both it and the in-memory copy. It's the
  cheapest option that actually satisfies "works across the app," and it fails closed when the tab closes.
